A middleware can stop a request from reaching the rest of the pipeline by **short‑circuiting** the pipeline.  
When a middleware decides not to call `next.Invoke`, it terminates the request and becomes a *terminal middleware*. This prevents any downstream middleware from executing.  

```csharp
app.Use(async (context, next) =>
{
    if (context.Request.Path.StartsWithSegments("/admin"))
    {
        // Stop the pipeline – do not call next()
        context.Response.StatusCode = 403;
        await context.Response.WriteAsync("Forbidden");
    }
    else
    {
        await next();   // Continue to the next middleware
    }
});
```

[1] describes how middleware can terminate the request, [2] explains that a `Run` delegate or not calling `next.Invoke` short‑circuits the pipeline, and [4] and [5] define terminal middleware as one that stops further processing.

