Use a **hosted service**.  
Add a class that implements `IHostedService` (or derives from `BackgroundService`) and register it in the DI container. The service’s `ExecuteAsync` (or `StartAsync` for short‑running setup) runs on a thread‑pool thread and can perform the long‑running work independently of HTTP requests. The host will start the service after the request pipeline is configured and will stop it gracefully when the application shuts down.

```csharp
public class MyLongRunningService : BackgroundService
{
    protected override async Task ExecuteAsync(CancellationToken stoppingToken)
    {
        while (!stoppingToken.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            // long‑running work here
            await Task.Delay(TimeSpan.FromMinutes(1), stoppingToken);
        }
    }
}

// In Program.cs
builder.Services.AddHostedService<MyLongRunningService>();
```

This follows the best‑practice recommendation to keep long‑running work out of the request pipeline and uses the built‑in hosted‑service infrastructure.

